# <%= it.model.app.name %>

A CRUD application over an RDF store, generated by the Dataspecer application generator from an
application graph. The pages, the data model and the wiring all come from that graph. Everything
under `src/shared/` is a runtime library copied in as ordinary source.

## Getting started

```sh
npm install
npm run dev
```

The application reads and writes through the SPARQL endpoint configured in
`src/config/data-sources.ts`. Point it somewhere else by editing that file. Nothing else needs to
change.

```sh
npm run build     # type-check, then bundle into dist/
npm run lint      # type-aware ESLint, including the rules of hooks
npm run preview   # serve the built bundle
```

## Structure

```
src/
  config/                 data source, aggregate registry, application settings
  modules/<name>/         one folder per data structure from the graph
    model.ts                TypeScript shape of an entity
    descriptor.ts           field metadata the forms and views read
    ldkit-schema.ts         RDF mapping used by the data source
    <route>-page.tsx        one page per graph node
    <route>-operation.ts    where you override that page's behaviour
  shared/                 the runtime library: components, forms, operations, data source
  routes.tsx              every route, each loading its page on demand
  App.tsx main.tsx        the router and the providers
```

A page owns its own title, navigation and strategy. Open `src/modules/<name>/<route>-page.tsx` to
see everything one page does.

## Where to change things

| To change | Edit |
| --- | --- |
| Data source | `src/config/data-sources.ts` |
| Languages initially shown in multilingual fields | `src/config/app-config.ts` |
| Colors, spacing, theming | `src/theme.ts` |
| What a page does before or after read/write | `src/modules/<name>/<route>-operation.ts` |
| Page title, actions or redirect | its `<route>-page.tsx` |
| How every list, form or detail view behaves | `src/shared/components/` |

## Yours to change, and keeping it

These are the places meant to be edited, ordered by how safely a change carries into the next
generated version:

- `src/modules/<name>/<route>-operation.ts`, where you override what a page does around a read or
  a write.
- `src/config/data-sources.ts`, where you swap the data source for your own.
- `src/shared/`, the runtime library, which is the same in every generated application. Changing it
  is the advanced case: it survives only while the API the generated code calls stays the same.

Everything else is written from the graph. Generating again overwrites every file, this one
included, so keep the application in version control and merge your changes back with a diff tool.
A structure that gains or loses fields changes the generated model and descriptor, and an override
written against the old shape has to be adapted.

## How it works

Lists use the MUI data grid with paging and single-column ordering done by the data source. Sorting
is offered for top-level single-valued fields, not for repeating or nested ones.

Forms are built from the field metadata in `descriptor.ts`. Entities composed into the edited one
are edited in place when they are simple, and open as their own pane when they compose something
themselves. The pane is in the address bar, so back and forward walk the nesting.

Multilingual fields keep all loaded language tags, including untagged text. The language selector
starts with the tags in `src/config/app-config.ts`.

Writes go from the leaves to the root. If one fails, its ancestors are not written, and the writes
that already succeeded are not rolled back. Removing a stored child from a form deletes it once the
parent no longer refers to it.

Deleting shows what else goes with it: the composed entities that will be deleted, and up to ten
resources elsewhere that still point at this one. The second list is a warning and does not block
the delete.
